1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:09,549
Pulse attributes. One derived value for every record, taken apart in depth.

2
00:00:10,349 --> 00:00:20,533
By default, attributes run third, after the filters, so they see only the records that survived. Groups, aggregations and windows can all read the new column.

3
00:00:21,333 --> 00:00:26,632
Each attribute names a type, a source and a label. The label becomes the new column.

4
00:00:27,432 --> 00:00:36,357
The label must be new. Reusing a field name, a feature column or an earlier label is refused, and predict catches it before anything runs.

5
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:50,626
Features add columns before the filters, for every record. Attributes add them after, for the survivors. Two exceptions come later: previous state runs before the filters, and some formulas run after the windows.

6
00:00:51,426 --> 00:00:59,562
Row-local attributes read only the record in front of them, so they finish in one pass and stream. So does any score of a bound fit.

7
00:01:00,362 --> 00:01:09,799
Two-pass attributes score a row against the whole column or a model. Pulse makes one pass to learn the statistics, then a second to score every row.

8
00:01:10,599 --> 00:01:20,058
Percentile rank and previous state are buffered. Percentile sorts the values first, and previous state orders each subject's records, so neither can stream.

9
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:30,082
A formula evaluates an expression for each row. Arithmetic on a null raises an error that stops the whole request, so guard it with a default.

10
00:01:30,882 --> 00:01:41,407
Categories read as their labels and sets read as lists. True becomes one and false becomes zero. An expression that does not compile is refused, at predict too.

11
00:01:42,207 --> 00:01:49,682
A formula can also call total, which sums an expression over every filtered row and hands the same figure to each one.

12
00:01:50,482 --> 00:01:54,117
That makes shares easy: each income divided by the total.

13
00:01:54,917 --> 00:02:02,733
A formula with total is two-pass, and total is only allowed in formula attributes, never in a filter or an overlay.

14
00:02:03,533 --> 00:02:13,461
Total is weight-aware. Under a frequency weight it sums the weight times the value. A probability weight is refused, because the total would scale with the weights.

15
00:02:14,261 --> 00:02:23,101
Attributes run in the order you list them, and a later one can read an earlier one's label: a formula, a z-score of it, then a formula of that.

16
00:02:23,901 --> 00:02:30,672
Pulse plans the passes for you. Each layer of two-pass attributes that depend on each other adds one more scan.

17
00:02:31,472 --> 00:02:40,653
A few attributes read source columns only, like code-in and the multivariate scores. To chain across requests, use Compose or ProcessChain.

18
00:02:41,453 --> 00:02:50,570
Z-score measures how many standard deviations a value sits from the mean, using the population mean and standard deviation of the filtered rows.

19
00:02:51,370 --> 00:02:57,159
T-score is the same idea on a friendlier scale: mean fifty, standard deviation ten.

20
00:02:57,959 --> 00:03:07,247
Normalized squeezes values into zero to one. Watch the outlier: one income of one hundred and forty pushes everyone else toward the bottom.

21
00:03:08,047 --> 00:03:12,962
Percentile gives each value its rank as a percentage. Ties never share a rank.

22
00:03:13,762 --> 00:03:22,815
Member ten has no income, so all four scores are null, and an aggregation counts them as missing. A field that never varies scores null too.

23
00:03:23,615 --> 00:03:31,964
Code-in flags whether a value is one of a set of codes, like the top two boxes of a rating scale. Its mean is the top-box share.

24
00:03:32,764 --> 00:03:39,620
A null counts as no. And if you filter to the codes first, the base shrinks, and the share reads one hundred percent.

25
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:47,276
Date part pulls out one calendar part. I S O weekday counts from Monday as one. A missing date gives null.

26
00:03:48,076 --> 00:03:55,316
Set-has and popcount read multi-select answers. A null set has no count, but an empty set counts zero.

27
00:03:56,116 --> 00:04:03,207
Previous state reads a long panel. For each record it finds the same subject's state at the previous time point.

28
00:04:04,007 --> 00:04:10,842
It is null on a subject's first time point and after a missing state. It never carries an older value forward.

29
00:04:11,642 --> 00:04:20,333
Unlike every other attribute, it runs over all the records before the filters. So a date window still sees each subject's true previous plan.

30
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:27,072
That also means an expression filter can read it, here to keep only the customers who changed plan.

31
00:04:27,872 --> 00:04:36,179
A row-local formula that reads a window label runs after the windows. Here an adstock window feeds a hill saturation curve.

32
00:04:36,979 --> 00:04:46,288
Predict shows the plan: the formula moves to a post-window phase, and the windows run over the records first. A regression predict can sit there too.

33
00:04:47,088 --> 00:04:52,472
Any other attribute that reads a window label is refused, at predict and at runtime.

34
00:04:53,272 --> 00:05:00,640
Regression attributes fit a least-squares model and give every row a diagnostic. Fitted is the model's prediction.

35
00:05:01,440 --> 00:05:08,765
Residual is the gap between the actual value and the prediction. Large residuals flag unusual responses.

36
00:05:09,565 --> 00:05:14,330
Leverage flags unusual predictor values, which pull hardest on the fit.

37
00:05:15,130 --> 00:05:23,479
Each slot refits its own model, or it can score a bound fit instead, without refitting. A null predictor gives null, never zero.

38
00:05:24,279 --> 00:05:31,967
Mahalanobis distance measures how far a row sits from the centre of several fields at once, allowing for how they move together.

39
00:05:32,767 --> 00:05:39,794
This member is tall but light. Each value looks ordinary on its own, but together they sit far outside the cloud.

40
00:05:40,594 --> 00:05:50,202
Principal-component and factor scores place each row on a fitted dimension. All three fit across the whole cohort, never per group, or score a bound fit.

41
00:05:51,002 --> 00:06:00,290
S E M score places each row on a latent variable of a structural equation model. With a group, each group scores with its own parameters.

42
00:06:01,090 --> 00:06:06,687
Scale score averages a rating battery, and returns null when too few items were answered.

43
00:06:07,487 --> 00:06:12,551
Vector distance and profile similarity compare each row with a reference profile.

44
00:06:13,351 --> 00:06:20,442
Here's what a null input becomes. Almost every attribute returns null, so an aggregation counts it as missing.

45
00:06:21,242 --> 00:06:28,226
Only the two membership flags, code-in and set-has, read zero, so their average counts a missing answer as no.

46
00:06:29,026 --> 00:06:38,741
That's the attribute toolkit today: formulas, standard scores, recodes, previous state, regression diagnostics, and multivariate and battery scores.

47
00:06:39,541 --> 00:06:49,405
More attributes are added over time, and embedders can register their own through Options dot Extensions. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

48
00:06:50,205 --> 00:06:55,866
Remember: check what a null becomes before you average it. Next up, the expression language.

49
00:06:56,666 --> 00:06:58,082
Pulse attributes.
